{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Then you shall send her away for herself</b>. They said in the Sifre (Ki Tetze 14): and not to her father's house. Now I do not know whether to say that she is a Jewess and they do not allow her to abandon the Torah of Israel and return to her people and her elohim, or to say that he must not send her to her father's house by way of a messenger's dispatch, for he would thereby assist the hands of transgressors, but rather he must put her out of his house to dwell wherever she may find, for perhaps she will remain in the land and go after the young men and be married to one of them. Now since Scripture says that he shall send her away \"for herself\" and warns that he shall not sell her for money and shall not deal tyrannically (תִתְעַמֵר) with her as a slave, we infer that she does not need a get (bill of divorce) from him, but Scripture made her a married woman while she was with him, and when he dislikes her he must send her away like a captive woman. And it is possible that the reason of Scripture is to say that if he has relations with her and desires her she shall be his wife, but if he has relations with her and she is not pleasing to him and he does not desire her — in the manner of Amnon with Tamar (II Samuel 13:15) — he shall send her away for herself. And everything depends on this act of intercourse: for if after her intercourse she remains with him for days and he comes upon her, and then he turns and hates her as a man who hates his wife, she has already become his wife, and she is a Jewess and is divorced with a get." "<b>You shall not deal tyrannically with her</b>, like וְהִתְעַמֵר בּוֹ וּמְכָרוֹ (Deuteronomy 24:7). Onkelos (Onkelos on Deuteronomy 21:14) renders it in the sense of commerce, and our Rabbis in the Sifre (Ki Tetze 16) and in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 85a) render it in the sense of sexual use. Now it is an expression of warning, that he must not make use of her at all as a slave, and if he does make use of her he receives lashes. And what seems right in my eyes according to the plain sense is that לֹא תִתְעַמֵר is like לֹא תִתְאַמֵר, from the expression יִתְאַמְרוּ כָּל פֹּעֲלֵי אָוֶן (Psalms 94:4), and likewise אֶת ה' הֶאֱמַרְתָּ הַיּוֹם (Deuteronomy 26:17), and likewise וּבִכְבוֹדָם תִּתְיַמָרוּ (Isaiah 61:6) with the exchange of aleph for yod, and all of them denote exaltation and greatness: הֶאֱמַרְתָּ הַיּוֹם — you exalted and magnified Him to be for you as elohim; הֶאֱמִירְךָ הַיּוֹם (Deuteronomy 26:18) — He exalts and magnifies you above all the peoples to be for Him as a treasured people; and all of them are derived from בְּרֹאשׁ אָמִיר (Isaiah 17:6). Scripture says: you shall not exalt yourself and magnify yourself over her, so that you are her master, to sell her and to make use of her as a slave. And so in Arabic they call a master amir, and the interchange of ayin and aleph as one is common in the language: פְּדָעֵהוּ מֵרֶדֶת שָׁחַת (Job 33:24) is פְּדָאֵהוּ, as I explained (on Numbers 12:4) regarding the word פֶּתַע, and likewise מַעֲשֵׂה צַעֲצוּעִים (II Chronicles 3:10) is as though with an aleph, from הַצֶּאֱצָאִים וְהַצְּפִעֹת (Isaiah 22:24); בָּנִים and בָּנוֹת, for the Targum of כְּרוּבִים is כְּרָבְיָא, and in the language of the Sages עוֹמְדוֹת becomes אוֹמְדוֹת, בְּקִיאִין becomes בְּקִיעִין, עֲדַיִן becomes אֱדַיִן, the attendants of the judges are אָרְכִי, עָרִיס becomes אָרִיס. And we learned (Shabbat 76a): milk enough for גְּמִיעָה, and they asked in the Gemara (ibid. 77a): גְּמִיעָה or גְּמִיאָה? And they resolved it from הַגְמִיאִינִי נָא מְעַט מַיִם (Genesis 24:17), yet even so they say of him: מְגַמֵּעַ — he does not גּוֹמֵעַ and spit out, but rather מְגַמֵּעַ and swallows (ibid. 111a). And in the Mishnah in tractate Kelim, in the Tosefta, we learned מַרְדָּא in place of מַרְדֵּעַ, and they are not particular at all about the interchange of these two letters, and likewise in many places. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Deuteronomy 21:14): לֹא תִתְעַמֵר בָּהּ — you shall not make use of her; in the Persian language they call labor and service amira; from the foundation of Rabbi Moses ha-Darshan I learned this. And this is true, for so I saw in the Yerushalmi: תַּמָּן צָוְחִין לְפָעֲלָא טָבָא עֲמִירָא, in the chapter \"Amar Rabbi Akiva\" (Shabbat 9:1) and in the chapter \"Kol ha-Tzelamim\" (Avodah Zarah 3:6), and it is current even in the Aramaic tongue as written in Megillat Shoshan: וְשַׁדַּר מַלְכָּא דְּאַתּוּר עַל כֻּלְּהוֹן עֲמִירָה דְּנִינְוֵה, and עַל כָּל דְּעָמְרִין עַל יַד יַמָּא, and עַל עֲמוּר דְּכַרְמְלָא וּדְגִלְעָד, and וְאַהְפִּיכוּ פִּתְגָמָא דִּנְבוּכַדְנֶצַּר כֻּלְּהוֹן עֲמוּרַיָּא דְּאַרְעָא וְלָא תִּדְחֲלוּן מִנֵּהּ. And likewise in many places in that book the inhabitants of the land are called <b>עֲמוּרֵי אַרְעָא</b>, meaning those who serve the land and make use of it, or else the king of Assyria commanded the men of war who are called <b>עַבְדֵי הָאָרֶץ</b>, for they are the ones who serve the king in his wars; they are his men of war and his horsemen, in the language of עָבְדוּ אֶת כְּדָרְלָעֹמֶר (Genesis 14:4), and עָבְדוּ בוֹ גּוֹיִם רַבִּים (Jeremiah 27:7)." "<b>Because you have afflicted her</b>. They said in the Sifre (Ki Tetze 16): even after an individual act. And this is what we mentioned above (Ramban on Deuteronomy 21:13), that they hold it is permitted to have first intercourse with her immediately upon bringing her into his house, and from that very moment he is warned concerning her regarding tyranny (עִמּוּר) and regarding sale."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "Ramban cites numerous Aramaic words from the Book of Esther (in the Megillah, 'Megillat Shoshan') where עֲמוּרַיָּא appears; the verse citations for these Aramaic phrases (e.g., Esther 3:12; 8:9; 10:1) are implicit in his references."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:29.010709019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}